<ul><li>{""}<a href={"https://www.stanford.edu/~boyd/cvxbook/bv_cvxbook.pdf"}>{"Boyd and Vandenberghe, "}<em>{"Convex Optimization"}</em>{", chapter 5"}</a>{". Sections 5.1–5.6 develop dual functions, geometric supporting bounds, optimality conditions and sensitivity. Compare the attainable residual/cost set with the perturbation epigraph when revisiting section 5 here; they answer different questions. The reading used these selected chapter sections, not the complete book."}</li><li>{""}<a href={"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-253-convex-analysis-and-optimization-spring-2012/pages/lecture-notes/"}>{"Bertsekas, MIT 6.253 lecture notes"}</a>{". Lecture 10 and the selected duality/qualification and initial sensitivity portions of lectures 11–12 offer a complementary route through minimax, domain geometry and supporting slopes. The relative-interior and conic extensions go beyond the simple whole-space assumptions used in the core argument here."}</li><li>{""}<a href={"https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/optimize.linprog-highs.html"}>{"SciPy "}<code>{"linprog"}</code>{", HiGHS method"}</a>{". Check argument shapes, status and RHS marginal conventions when adapting the ordinary LP route. The complete example explicitly sets bounds and maps returned marginals back to the original demand inequalities."}</li></ul>

